<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">OAU Liberation Committee</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1963–1994</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as African Liberation Committee, Liberation Committee of the Organisation of African Unity</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The OAU Liberation Committee was the body set up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> at its founding in 1963 to coordinate and fund the liberation movements fighting colonial and white-minority rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a>, it supported movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, Rhodesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and was dissolved in 1994 after South Africa's first democratic elections.</p></div>
